deploying.md: the Gemfile error now means the Root directory field
The document was contradicting itself, and the stale half is the one you would
read while fighting that error.

## What was wrong

The settings table says **Root directory: `site`**. Twenty lines later the
troubleshooting entry for `Could not locate Gemfile` said:

> **This is not the Root directory setting.** `/` is correct and is what
> `lesbistrology` uses successfully. Look at the GitHub App's access to the
> repository instead.

That was true until 2026-09-18, when the build root moved into `site/` and the
`Gemfile` went with it. **It is now the first thing to check**, and the old text
was actively sending you to look at GitHub App permissions for a misconfigured
field.

## Two causes, one error message

Told apart by a detail already in the log:

| | |
|---|---|
| **Root directory is not `site`** | `Detected the following tools from environment:` **names Ruby** — the image found the repo, just not the Gemfile. The likely cause today |
| **Checkout was empty or partial** | that line is **empty**. Bundler ran, so the image was fine; there was no repository under it. Then it is GitHub App access |

`lesbistrology` is dropped as a comparison: its site is at its repository root
and this one's is not, so it stopped being evidence about this field.

## Root directory vs build watch paths

Separated, because changing one while meaning the other looks like a setting
that did not take. Root directory is *where the build runs* — wrong gives the
Gemfile error. Watch paths is *what triggers a build* — wrong gives no build,
not a failed one.

**Watching `site` only is correct**, and for a reason worth having written down:
the factory writes into `site/member-sites/`, so a cadence run that publishes or
delists a member touches `site/` and triggers a deploy like any content change.
Delisting takes a site down through that same path.

It will not rebuild for root-level changes alone — `member-site-core/`,
`sites.yml`, `bin/`, the docs. That is right rather than a gap: a restyle of the
core changes nothing published until the factory has been re-run, and that run is
what touches `site/`.

## The failure-then-success pair

Recorded as most likely the setting being changed between the two runs. But
**two project configurations pointed at one repository look identical from
here**, and that is worth ruling out in the dashboard — the failing one keeps
failing on every push, and its red badge is indistinguishable from a real
regression.

## Verified

- `python3 site/bin/test_no_secrets.py` — 12 tests, OK (2 skipped).
- Documents only. Nothing under `site/` changed.

